{
  "design": {
    "theme": "studio",
    "picture_style": "illustration"
  },
  "flow": [
    {
      "slide": 1,
      "does": "Recall everyday experiences of remembering",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "picture",
        "shows": "A pupil remembering a briefly seen door code"
      },
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "does": "Introduce the approved objectives",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "does": "Describe and compare the three stores",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Coding, capacity and duration across the stores"
      },
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "does": "Explain transfers and maintenance rehearsal",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Attention, rehearsal and retrieval linking the stores"
      },
      "teaches": [2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 5,
      "does": "Model an explanation of remembering a code",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "A code moving through the memory stores"
      },
      "teaches": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "does": "Apply the model to a near-identical example",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "does": "Correct the belief that STM only lasts 30 seconds",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Rehearsal maintaining information within STM"
      },
      "teaches": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 8,
      "does": "Check understanding of rehearsal and duration",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 9,
      "does": "Explain serial position findings as supporting evidence",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "Immediate and delayed recall in Glanzer and Cunitz"
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 10,
      "does": "Explain KF as a challenge to one STM store",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "diagram",
        "shows": "The contrast between KF’s verbal and visual STM"
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 11,
      "does": "Model a linked evaluation paragraph",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 12,
      "does": "Evaluate independently and apply the model to new evidence",
      "look_at": {
        "kind": "none",
        "shows": null
      },
      "teaches": [2, 3]
    }
  ],
  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "The multi-store model of memory",
    "lead": "Recall a time you forgot a code but remembered a familiar address. What helped one memory last?",
    "picture": {
      "shows": "A pupil looking away from a briefly seen door keypad",
      "must_see": ["door keypad", "pupil looking away"],
      "subject": "generic"
    }
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Three stores, three sets of properties",
      "lead": "Coding is the form of information; capacity is how much fits; duration is how long it lasts.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The typical properties of the stores in Atkinson and Shiffrin’s model",
        "alt": "A table compares coding, capacity and duration in sensory memory, short-term memory and long-term memory.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Store", "Coding", "Capacity", "Duration"],
        "rows": [
          ["Sensory register", "Sense-specific", "Very large", "Very brief"],
          ["Short-term memory", "Mainly acoustic", "About 7 ± 2 items", "About 18–30 seconds"],
          ["Long-term memory", "Mainly semantic", "Potentially unlimited", "Potentially lifelong"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "How information moves",
      "lead": "Atkinson and Shiffrin proposed separate stores in 1968.",
      "points": [
        "Attention selects sensory information for STM.",
        "Maintenance rehearsal repeats information in STM.",
        "Prolonged rehearsal transfers it to LTM; retrieval returns it to STM."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The model’s transfers and rehearsal loop; information can also be lost from each store",
        "alt": "Attention links the sensory register to STM. Rehearsal loops within STM and transfers information to LTM. Retrieval returns it to STM.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Sensory register", "STM", "LTM"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 1, "label": "Attention"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 1, "label": "Maintenance rehearsal"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 2, "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"},
          {"from": 2, "to": 1, "label": "Retrieval"}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Worked example: a door code",
      "lead": "Mia sees 4826, repeats it silently and later recalls it.",
      "points": [
        "The visual trace enters the sensory register.",
        "Attention puts it in STM; silent repetition maintains its acoustic code.",
        "The model predicts prolonged rehearsal stores it in LTM; later recall uses retrieval."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The processes used to remember a briefly seen door code",
        "alt": "A visual trace is attended to, repeated in STM and stored in LTM before later retrieval.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Visual trace", "Code in STM", "Code in LTM"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 1, "label": "Attention"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 1, "label": "Repetition"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 2, "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"},
          {"from": 2, "to": 1, "label": "Retrieval"}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Explain a second code",
      "questions": [
        "Leo sees 7392 and focuses on it. Name the first two stores involved and the process linking them.",
        "He repeats the code silently. Explain what this does and its likely coding in STM.",
        "After prolonged repetition, he recalls it the next day. Explain this using the model."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write a short explanation for each stage.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Does STM always stop after 30 seconds?",
      "lead": "The duration estimate applies when rehearsal is prevented.",
      "points": [
        {
          "label": "Counterexample",
          "text": "Repeating a code can keep it available in STM for longer."
        },
        {
          "label": "Distinction",
          "text": "Keeping information active in STM is not the same as storing it in LTM."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "A rehearsal loop maintaining a code within STM without requiring a transfer to LTM",
        "alt": "A code in STM has a maintenance rehearsal loop returning to the same store.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Code in STM", "LTM"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 0, "label": "Maintenance rehearsal"}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "hinge",
      "heading": "Choose the best explanation",
      "stem": "Sam repeats a new code for 45 seconds and can still say it. What follows?",
      "options": [
        "It must be in LTM because 30 seconds have passed.",
        "Rehearsal may be keeping it active in STM.",
        "Attention is keeping it in the sensory register.",
        "STM has unlimited duration without rehearsal."
      ],
      "correct": 2
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Glanzer and Cunitz: separate stores?",
      "lead": "In 1966, participants recalled word lists immediately or after a filled 30-second delay.",
      "points": [
        "Immediate recall favoured early words (primacy) and final words (recency).",
        "The delay reduced recency but largely preserved primacy.",
        "Early words had more rehearsal into LTM; final words relied on STM."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "A schematic serial position pattern, not original numerical data: immediate recall has primacy and recency, while a filled delay reduces recency",
        "alt": "Schematic curves show good recall of early words in both conditions, but good recall of final words only with immediate recall.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {"label": "Position in word list", "min": 1, "max": 10, "step": 1},
        "y": {"label": "Recall", "min": 0, "max": 1, "step": null},
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": true,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "Immediate",
            "points": [[1, 0.85], [2, 0.7], [3, 0.5], [4, 0.35], [5, 0.3], [6, 0.3], [7, 0.4], [8, 0.6], [9, 0.8], [10, 0.95]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          },
          {
            "label": "Filled delay",
            "points": [[1, 0.85], [2, 0.7], [3, 0.5], [4, 0.35], [5, 0.3], [6, 0.3], [7, 0.3], [8, 0.3], [9, 0.3], [10, 0.3]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": null,
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "KF: is STM one store?",
      "lead": "Shallice and Warrington studied KF, a patient with brain damage, in 1970.",
      "points": [
        "His verbal STM was severely impaired; his visual STM was relatively preserved.",
        "Different performance across types of information challenges one uniform STM store.",
        "A single unusual patient limits generalisation."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The contrasting pattern of short-term memory performance in KF",
        "alt": "KF had severely impaired verbal short-term memory but relatively preserved visual short-term memory.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["STM task", "KF’s performance"],
        "rows": [
          ["Verbal", "Severely impaired"],
          ["Visual", "Relatively preserved"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "steps",
      "heading": "Worked evaluation: link evidence to a claim",
      "points": [
        "Claim: serial position findings support separate STM and LTM stores.",
        "Evidence: a filled delay reduced recall of final words but largely preserved recall of early words.",
        "Link: the delay disrupted STM information, while rehearsed early words remained in LTM; this supports separation, not every part of the model."
      ],
      "figure": null
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Build an argument, then test the model",
      "questions": [
        "KF had severely impaired verbal STM but relatively preserved visual STM. Write a claim–evidence–link paragraph challenging one STM store.",
        "A new study finds that a filled delay reduces recall of final words but preserves recall of early words. Explain how the model accounts for this.",
        "Stretch: a pupil remembers the meaning of a message without repeating its exact words. Explain why this may challenge the model’s emphasis on rehearsal."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write independently. Use evidence to justify each conclusion.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
